/* book Worked example 2: Q: Treat a red cell as a sphere of diameter 8 µm and density 1100 kg/m³ in plasma of density 1025 kg/m³ and viscosity 1.2 × 10⁻³ Pa·s. Find its settling speed (a) under gravity and (b) at 1500 × g.
   1. Δρ = 1100 − 1025 = 75 kg/m³; d = 8 × 10⁻⁶ m, so d² = 6.4 × 10⁻¹¹ m².
   2. (a) Under gravity: v = d²Δρg/(18η) = 6.4 × 10⁻¹¹ × 75 × 9.81 / (18 × 1.2 × 10⁻³) = 4.71 × 10⁻⁸ / 0.0216 = 2.18 × 10⁻⁶ m/s.
   3. That is 2.18 × 10⁻⁶ × 3600 = 7.8 × 10⁻³ m per hour, about 8 mm per hour.
   4. (b) At 1500 × g the field is 1500 × 9.81 = 14 715 m/s², so v = 1500 × 2.18 × 10⁻⁶ = 3.3 × 10⁻³ m/s, about 3.3 mm per second.
   5. A single cell would cross a 5 cm column in 50 / 3.3 ≈ 15 s; in whole blood the cells crowd and hinder each other, so packing takes minutes, which is why serum spins last about 10 min.
   Answer: (a) About 2.2 µm/s, or 8 mm per hour, under gravity. (b) About 3.3 mm/s at 1500 × g, 1500 times faster. */
