/* book exam traps: Forgetting to subtract the background count, or reporting raw counts instead of counts per second. | Claiming the GM pulse height tells you the radiation energy — it does not; energy information comes from detectors whose pulse height follows deposited energy, such as the scintillation counter, proportional counter or a semiconductor detector. | Mixing the units: curie is activity (1 Ci = 3.7 × 10¹⁰ Bq), gray is absorbed dose, sievert is equivalent dose. | Treating dead time as microseconds of no consequence: at 5000 counts per second with 200 µs dead time the tube is effectively saturated. | Setting the GM voltage anywhere on the plateau without checking it is a flat region — below it counts are lost, above it the tube discharges continuously. */
